---
// Approved wording: ops site/101-page-copy.md (4 Nov 2026). Bracketed items there are gaps, not copy.
// Paid booking stays closed until delivery, demo, payment and setup readiness are verified,
// so "Book a seat" renders as an unavailable control with no destination.
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import CourseFigure from "../../components/CourseFigure.astro";
import { ENQUIRY } from "../../config/enquiry.mjs";
import "../../styles/course.css";

const { email } = ENQUIRY;
const price = "Price on request";
const bookingStatus = "Booking isn't open yet.";
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Power BI with AI agents 101 | burningsuit"
  description="Half a day, live online. You get an agent to explain a prepared report you've never seen and write notes on its calculations."
  placeholder={false}
>
  <div class="dark stage">
  <div class="title-band wrap">
    <h1 id="page-title">Power BI with AI <span class="em">agents</span> 101</h1>
    <p class="standfirst">Want to be ready for where Power BI is going?</p>
  </div>
  </div>
  <div class="course-opening wrap">
    <section class="course-intro" aria-labelledby="page-title">
      <p>You've probably heard that AI agents are changing how Power BI reports get built. Join me for a morning to take your first steps in agentic development. You get an agent to explain a prepared report you've never seen and write notes on its calculations. Then you have it build one new calculation, and you check its answers yourself.</p>
      <p>By the end, you can judge where Power BI is going from your own hands-on use, not from what you've read about it.</p>
      <p class="course-fit">If you've built a report in Power BI Desktop, even a simple one, you're ready. No coding or AI experience needed. If you already use coding agents on Power BI work every day, this covers ground you know.</p>
    </section>

    <aside class="course-run" aria-label="Course facts and booking">
      <ul class="run-facts" role="list">
        <li>Half a day, live online</li>
        <li><time datetime="2027-01-20">Wednesday 20 January 2027</time>, 9:30am to 12:30pm UK time</li>
        <li>4 to 8 people</li>
        <li>{price}</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="booking">
        <button class="button book-button" type="button" disabled aria-describedby="booking-status-opening">Book a seat</button>
        <p class="booking-status" id="booking-status-opening">{bookingStatus}</p>
      </div>
      <p class="private-ask">Want this for your team? <a href={`mailto:${email}`}>Ask about a private course.</a></p>
    </aside>
  </div>

  <section class="course-do" aria-labelledby="do-title">
    <div class="wrap do-inner">
      <div class="do-lead">
        <h2 id="do-title">What you'll do</h2>
        <p>One prepared report runs through the whole morning.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="do-body">
        <ol class="do-steps" role="list">
          <li><h3>Get your bearings.</h3><p>You see what an agent is, how a Power BI report's files fit together and what to check before giving a tool access to work files. Then you point your agent (GitHub Copilot, Claude or ChatGPT/Codex) at the teaching report.</p></li>
          <li><h3>Understand a report you didn't build.</h3><p>You ask the agent to explain the report: what each page shows, the tables behind it, how they connect, how the data is prepared and what each calculation does. Nothing changes yet.</p></li>
          <li><h3>Document its calculations.</h3><p>You give the agent a short set of house rules, then have it add comments and descriptions to the existing calculations, so whoever opens the report next can see what each one does. You bring the changes back into Power BI Desktop and check them there.</p></li>
          <li><h3>Build one new calculation and check it.</h3><p>You describe a new calculation and have the agent build it. Then you check its total, a filtered result and an awkward case. If an answer is wrong, you show the agent the evidence so it can put it right, and you can always go back to a clean copy.</p></li>
        </ol>
        <p class="do-next">We finish with where this goes next: tools that let an agent check its own work, and agents doing more of the building while you decide what's right.</p>
        {/* Reserved: one showcase line, held until that separately managed work supplies its wording and destination. */}
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="wrap"><CourseFigure kind="agent" /></div>
  </section>

  <section class="course-trust wrap" aria-labelledby="trust-title">
    <div class="trust-check">
      <h2 id="trust-title">How you'll check its answers</h2>
      <p>An agent can sound sure of itself and still be wrong. You learn to check its work against numbers you can confirm, correct it with evidence and go back to a clean copy when you need to. Deciding whether an answer is right stays with you.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="trust-teacher">
      <h3>Who's teaching</h3>
      <p>I'm Alan Harman-Box. I've spent about five years working with organisations as they adopt Power BI.</p>
      {/* Gap: Alan's own use of agents on Power BI work, held until its entry is added to the private reference sheet in his words. */}
      <a class="text-link" href="/training/how-i-train/">How I train</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="course-need wrap" aria-labelledby="need-title">
    <h2 id="need-title">What you'll need</h2>
    <div class="need-body">
      <ul class="need-list" role="list">
        <li>A Windows computer with Power BI Desktop <span class="to-confirm">version and settings: to confirm</span>.</li>
        <li>One of three agent tools, on a paid plan: GitHub Copilot, Claude or ChatGPT/Codex <span class="to-confirm">which app and plan, and what it costs: to confirm</span>.</li>
        <li>Permission to install and use that tool. On a work laptop, check with your IT team early.</li>
        <li>A folder on your computer for the teaching files <span class="to-confirm">where: to confirm</span>, reliable internet and audio. A second screen helps.</li>
        <li>A short setup check before the day <span class="to-confirm">when, and how long it takes: to confirm</span>.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="need-note">You work on a prepared report and sample data, not your organisation's own reports.</p>
      <p class="keep">Afterwards, you keep a recording of the morning, along with the teaching project and clean copies, so you can work through it again.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="course-book closing-band dark" aria-labelledby="book-title">
    <div class="wrap closing-inner">
      <div>
        <h2 id="book-title">Book a seat</h2>
        <p class="book-facts"><time datetime="2027-01-20">Wednesday 20 January 2027</time>, 9:30am to 12:30pm UK time, live online. 4 to 8 people. {price}.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="booking">
          <button class="button book-button" type="button" disabled aria-describedby="booking-status-book">Book a seat</button>
          <p class="booking-status" id="booking-status-book">{bookingStatus}</p>
        </div>
        <p class="invoice">If your organisation needs an invoice or a purchase order, email <a href={`mailto:${email}`}>{email}</a> and I'll send an invoice instead.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
